update public.articles set title_en = 'A premature baby''s corrected age: how to work it out, and how long to use it', summary_en = 'For a baby born early, two ages coexist: the actual age, counted from birth, and the corrected age, counted from the due date. How to calculate it, why it guides developmental follow-up until age 2, and what does not change (vaccines).', body_md_en = '## In brief
- A baby is premature when born **before 37 weeks of amenorrhoea (WA, counted from the last period)**; term is set at 40 weeks (SOS Préma, HAS).
- The **corrected age** is the age your baby would be if they had been born at term: it is counted from the **due date**, not from the birth (SOS Préma).
- It is used to judge milestones (motor skills, alertness) fairly. The HAS (French health authority) recommends taking it into account **until 2 years of actual age** when looking for developmental warning signs.
- **Vaccines**, on the other hand, are given at the **actual age**, without delay (SOS Préma).

## Understanding
**Three ages for one baby.** SOS Préma distinguishes gestational age (the number of weeks of pregnancy at birth, which is fixed), actual or chronological age (counted from birth) and corrected age, also called post-menstrual age, "the age the child should be if they had been born at term". The mpedia paediatrician sums it up: the corrected age corresponds to the gestational age, and it is used to judge the premature baby''s motor milestones.

**The calculation.** Subtract from the actual age the number of weeks missing up to 40 weeks.
- Mpedia''s example: a little girl born at 34 weeks, now almost 7 weeks old, has a corrected age of 41 weeks, i.e. **one week** of corrected age.
- Another example: a baby born at 32 weeks is 8 weeks "early". At 5 months of actual age, their corrected age is about 3 months.
- The simplest way: note the due date (it is in the pregnancy file) and count your baby''s age from that date.

**Why it is useful.** A baby born two months early has not had those two months of maturation: comparing their smile, head control or sitting with those of a term baby of the same actual age would be unfair and a source of worry. SOS Préma indicates that the corrected age serves as a reference for following development during the initial phase. The HAS specifies that, for a child born before 37 weeks, screening for warning signs takes into account the age relative to term **until 2 years of chronological age**.

**Degrees of prematurity** (SOS Préma): simple prematurity from 32 weeks to 36 weeks + 6 days, severe prematurity from 28 weeks to 31 weeks + 6 days, extreme prematurity before 28 weeks. The HAS classes severe prematurity among the high-risk factors for neurodevelopmental disorders, and moderate (32–33 weeks) and late (34–36 weeks) prematurity among moderate risks, which determines the type of follow-up offered.

## Day to day
**Using the corrected age without getting lost**
- Write both dates (birth and due date) on the first page of the carnet de santé (the child''s health record book) and tell every new professional.
- For developmental milestones (holding the head, rolling over, sitting), play and early-learning equipment, think in corrected age.
- For vaccines, think in actual age: SOS Préma points out that the 11 compulsory vaccines are given according to actual age, without delay; for babies born before 32 weeks, the first injection is often given before leaving the neonatal unit.
- For feeding (introducing solids), the timing is decided with the paediatrician, who takes both ages into account.

**Follow-up** (SOS Préma, HAS)
- A premature baby has specific follow-up by a doctor or a service with expertise in the development of premature children: a premature-baby follow-up network (for those under 32 weeks and vulnerable newborns), a CAMSP (early medico-social action centre), the neonatal team, or a community paediatrician taking part in a network.
- For high-risk children, the HAS recommends standardised screening tests at 9, 18, 24 and 30–36 months, then at 4 and 5 years, and specific autism screening at 18 and 24 months. For moderate risks, a screening consultation with the family doctor, the PMI (mother-and-child health service) or the school doctor.
- Depending on their needs, the child may be supported by a physiotherapist, a psychomotor therapist, a speech therapist, an ophthalmologist or a psychologist.

**Alertness.** Mpedia indicates that the same activities as for a term baby are suitable, while respecting their great need for sleep: short stimulation, long calm periods, and cuddles. To observe their gaze, position yourself close to them when they are awake and calm, and move very slowly.

## Frequently asked questions
**How long do we talk in corrected age?**
The HAS uses it until 2 years of actual age for screening for warning signs. Beyond that, professionals mostly think in actual age, while keeping the prematurity in mind.

**My 6-month-old does not sit up. Should I worry?**
First count their corrected age: born at 30 weeks, they are about 3 and a half months in corrected age, an age at which sitting is not expected. Put the question to the follow-up network doctor, who will assess their development as a whole.

**Does the corrected age change the dates of the health-record examinations?**
The compulsory examinations follow the usual schedule; the HAS indicates that warning signs are looked for there with correction for premature babies until age 2.

**Should we correct for "simple" prematurity (35–36 weeks)?**
The HAS provides for correction for any child born before 37 weeks. The difference is small but real during the first months.

## When to seek advice
Outside scheduled visits, SOS Préma advises seeing a doctor, especially in the first year, in case of fever, cough, wheezing, vomiting, refusal to feed or unusual behaviour. Tell the follow-up network doctor about any concern over gaze, muscle tone, asymmetrical movements, absence of smiling or babbling at the expected corrected age. Difficulty breathing, pallor or unusual drowsiness mean calling **15** or **112**.

_This article is for information only; it does not replace the advice of a healthcare professional (doctor, midwife, dentist, paediatrician, psychologist) or, for administrative procedures, that of your town hall (mairie) or a legal adviser._' where slug = 'age-corrige-bebe-premature-calcul-jusqua-quand-suivi';
update public.articles set title_en = 'Breastfeeding or expressing milk when you go back to work: your rights, organisation and storing milk', summary_en = 'One hour a day to breastfeed or express milk for one year, a compulsory room in companies with more than 100 employees, a breast pump covered on prescription, and precise storage times: what the law and paediatricians provide for continuing to breastfeed after maternity leave.', body_md_en = '## In brief
- The French Labour Code allows an employee to breastfeed or express milk during working hours **for one year from the birth**: **one hour a day**, in two 30-minute breaks, morning and afternoon (Service-Public.fr).
- These breaks are **not paid**, unless a more favourable collective agreement applies. There is no legal "breastfeeding leave" after maternity leave.
- From **100 employees**, the employer must provide a **breastfeeding room**; below that, the employee must be able to rest lying down in decent conditions.
- Expressed milk keeps **48 hours in the fridge** (4 °C or less) and **4 months in the freezer** (−18 °C); it is transported in a cool bag without exceeding 15 °C (mpedia, based on Anses, the French food safety agency).

## Understanding
**Three ways to organise breastfeeding after going back** (mpedia):
1. **Exclusive breastfeeding by expressing milk** at work, to cover the feeds given by the nursery or childminder. The WHO recommends exclusive breastfeeding until 6 months; mpedia adds that, for mothers under heavy constraints, 4 months of exclusive breastfeeding remain very beneficial.
2. **Mixed feeding**: you keep the feeds when you are present (morning, evening, weekends, holidays) and infant formula replaces the others, following the doctor''s advice. A few days of adjustment are generally enough; watch for engorgement, which is relieved by hand or with a pump.
3. **Gradual weaning**, started about 3 weeks before going back, total or partial.

Mpedia points out that all these options are possible, that the choice depends on your preferences and constraints, and that even if breastfeeding stops, the first months have already given your baby the essentials.

**Your rights** (Service-Public.fr, articles L1225-30 to L1225-33 and R4152-13 onwards of the Labour Code):
- a breastfeeding break of **one hour a day**, split into two 30-minute periods; without an agreement with the employer, they fall in the middle of each half-day;
- if the employer provides a **dedicated room** in or near the premises, the time is reduced to **20 minutes**;
- these breaks are not paid, unless a collective agreement provides for it;
- in companies with **100 employees or more**, the room must be separate from the work areas, ventilated, lit, clean, with a water point nearby and suitable seating; the labour inspector can require it to be set up;
- below 100 employees, no room is compulsory, but you must be able to rest lying down in decent conditions, and breastfeed on site or outside.

These rules apply to employees; civil servants come under their own regulations: ask your human resources department.

## Day to day
**Before going back**
- Talk to your employer or HR a few weeks in advance: timing of breaks, place, access to a fridge. Mpedia presents remote working, even a few days a week, as a valuable help.
- Ask for a **prescription** for a breast pump: mpedia indicates that hire is covered by the Assurance Maladie (French national health insurance) when the equipment is prescribed (maternity unit, doctor, midwife), provided the prescription is renewed. An electric double pump is recommended for regular use.
- Build up small reserves (60 to 90 ml) to freeze, once lactation is well established, i.e. about a month after the birth (mpedia).
- Offer the bottle or a cup to your baby with someone other than you, a few days beforehand.

**At work**
- Wash your hands thoroughly before each session; mpedia indicates that a double-pumping session does not exceed 20 minutes.
- If the milk stops flowing after about ten minutes, mpedia suggests restarting the let-down reflex by hand or with the pump''s "stimulation" mode. A photo or video of your baby can help.
- Keep the milk in the fridge at work, then transport it in a cool bag with an ice pack without exceeding 15 °C: the important thing is never to break the cold chain.

**Storage** (mpedia, based on Anses)
- Room temperature (19 to 22 °C): **1 hour maximum**.
- Fridge (4 °C or less): **48 hours**.
- Freezer (−18 °C): **4 months**; freeze within 24 hours of expressing, after cooling in the fridge, in glass or polypropylene containers filled three-quarters full and dated.
- Thawed milk: in the fridge, then to be used within 24 hours; never refreeze.
- Warm in a bain-marie or bottle warmer, never in the microwave; give within half an hour, and throw away the rest.

## Frequently asked questions
**My employer refuses breastfeeding breaks.**
They are provided for by the Labour Code for one year from the birth. Remind them of the rules in writing; if things stall, the labour inspectorate and staff representatives can help you.

**My milk supply has dropped since going back.**
This is common at first, while lactation adapts to the new rhythm. Morning, evening and weekend feeds, regular expressing sessions and enough rest all help. A lactation consultant, a midwife or the PMI (mother-and-child health service) can support you.

**My baby refuses the bottle at nursery.**
Many babies make up for it with more frequent feeds in the evening and at night. Some settings offer a cup or a spoon. Talk to the team, and to the doctor if weight gain worries you.

## When to seek advice
See your midwife, doctor or the PMI in case of a red, hot and painful breast with fever, cracked nipples that do not heal, a drop in milk supply that worries you, or if your baby wets fewer nappies, seems constantly hungry or is not gaining weight. For your rights, your HR department, the labour inspectorate or your health insurance office can inform you.

_This article is for information only; it does not replace the advice of a healthcare professional (doctor, midwife, dentist, paediatrician, psychologist) or, for administrative procedures, that of your town hall (mairie) or a legal adviser._' where slug = 'allaiter-ou-tirer-son-lait-reprise-du-travail-droits-organisation-conservation';
update public.articles set title_en = 'Explaining an appointment or a medical test to your child, without lying or dramatising', summary_en = 'Blood test, vaccine, dentist, hospital: the words that prepare without frightening, what you should never say ("it won''t hurt"), your place during the procedure and what helps afterwards, according to the SPARADRAP association and Santé publique France.', body_md_en = '## In brief
- Explain **why** the test is necessary, **what** it is for and **how** it will go, in words suited to their age; SPARADRAP (a French association for children facing healthcare) insists: "always avoid lying and playing it down".
- Do not say "it won''t hurt" or "don''t be scared": the child mainly remembers "hurt" and "scared" (SPARADRAP).
- Your **presence** during the procedure is often possible and desirable; there are ways to **reduce pain** (an anaesthetic patch for a blood test or vaccine, breastfeeding or a sugar solution for a baby).
- Afterwards, **comfort and praise**, even if everything went well, and let your child play the scene out again.

## Understanding
**Why the truth reassures.** A child who was promised "nothing at all" and who feels a needle learns that adults cannot be trusted: the next time will be harder. SPARADRAP recommends taking their experience into account: if it is a first time, make sure it goes as well as possible; if previous experiences were difficult, take their apprehension seriously instead of brushing it aside.

**What helps, by age.**
- *Baby*: Santé.fr advises speaking softly without showing worry, holding them sitting on your lap or in your arms, breastfeeding if possible or, failing that, giving a sugar solution at least 2 minutes before the procedure; a cuddly toy, a toy or music also help.
- *Toddler (1–3 years)*: simple explanations just before, a familiar object, your voice and your touch.
- *From 3–4 years*: explain the day before or the same morning, with a doll, a puppet, a suitable book or video (SPARADRAP); around age 4, children like to act out the examination with a doctor''s kit.
- *Older child*: explain what will happen, suggest breathing calmly and deeply at the moment of the procedure, then praise them for their courage (Santé.fr).

**Pain can be prevented.** SPARADRAP points out that teams have means at their disposal, even for procedures that are not very painful but are impressive. For a blood test, a dressing containing a cream that "numbs" the skin is applied at least an hour beforehand; Santé.fr indicates that the doctor can offer an anaesthetic patch an hour before a vaccine. Ask for it when the test is prescribed or when booking.

## Day to day
**Before**
- Choose when to tell them according to their age: shortly before for little ones, a day or two before for older children, to leave room for questions without letting anxiety grow.
- Describe the concrete steps. For a blood test, for example (SPARADRAP): a tight elastic band on the arm, something cold on the skin to clean it, a very thin needle, "it doesn''t last very long", then cotton wool and a plaster; very little blood is taken, like a few drops from a full bottle, and the body makes more.
- Say what they may feel: "it stings a bit", "it squeezes", "it''s cold". And what they can do: look or turn their head, squeeze your hand, blow, count.
- Encourage their questions, without pressing if they do not want to know more (SPARADRAP).
- For the dentist, play at counting teeth and opening wide; for the hospital, pack the bag together with the cuddly toy and a game.

**During**
- Stay beside them if you can: SPARADRAP indicates that parents'' presence is often possible and desirable, and that no regulation forbids it; if you do not want to or cannot stay, that is your right, without guilt.
- Reassure and distract: hold their hand, talk, sing, read, blow bubbles, count backwards.
- Let them express themselves without speaking for them; do not deny what they feel.
- If you are asked to hold them, do it gently, without pinning them down. If the child does not calm down, stopping the procedure to try again in better conditions is sometimes possible, unless the care is urgent.
- Do not scold them, do not threaten to leave if they cry, and do not ask them to "agree": it is up to the adults to carry the decision about the care (SPARADRAP).

**Afterwards**
- Comfort and praise, even if everything went well.
- Ask what they liked or did not like, what would have helped: their experience may differ from yours.
- Let them replay the scene with a soft toy or a drawing.

**Phrases to avoid**: "it won''t hurt", "don''t be scared", "if you move it''ll be worse", "the doctor will give you an injection if you''re naughty". Care must never be used as a threat.

## Frequently asked questions
**My child has been terrified of needles since a bad experience.**
Tell the team when you book: anaesthetic patch, distraction, parent present, sitting on your lap. SPARADRAP indicates that solutions exist to reduce fear, and that the patch is very effective against the pain of a blood test.

**Should I warn them that they will be admitted to hospital?**
Yes, in simple, true words, without distressing details: where they will sleep, who will be with them, what they can bring. SPARADRAP offers children''s leaflets for each planned procedure or operation.

**Can I give them something for the pain before a vaccine?**
Santé.fr indicates that there is no point giving a medicine before vaccination, as the injection only lasts a few seconds; ask the doctor whether they offer an anaesthetic patch.

## When to seek advice
Talk to the doctor if fear of care becomes overwhelming (refusal of any examination, nightmares, anxiety before every appointment), if your child has been through a traumatic procedure, or if a chronic illness requires repeated procedures: support from a psychologist or specific preparation at the hospital can help. After a procedure, see a doctor if a bruise swells a lot, if pain persists or if the puncture site becomes red, hot and painful.

_This article is for information only; it does not replace the advice of a healthcare professional (doctor, midwife, dentist, paediatrician, psychologist) or, for administrative procedures, that of your town hall (mairie) or a legal adviser._' where slug = 'expliquer-rendez-vous-examen-medical-enfant-sans-mentir-ni-dramatiser';
update public.articles set title_en = 'From the cot to a "big bed": when and how to make the move', summary_en = 'No set age, but some landmarks: the child who climbs out, the cot designed for use up to age 3, bedding after age 2, a safe bedroom and the steps that help your child take to their new bed.', body_md_en = '## In brief
- There is no fixed age: the paediatricians at mpedia (the website of the French association of community paediatricians) say the **cot** is used from birth to **about 3 years**, and the Réseau Morphée sleep network advises removing the bars **as soon as the child starts climbing over them**.
- Before age 2, mpedia advises against sheets, blankets, duvets and pillows: the sleeping bag remains the reference. So the change of bed is usually prepared **after age 2**.
- **High beds** and bunk beds are not suitable before **age 6** (DGCCRF, Réseau Morphée).
- The secret of a smooth transition: a calm period, an unchanged routine and a child-proofed bedroom, since your child will now be able to get up on their own.

## Understanding
**Why change beds.** The cot protects a child who cannot yet get down by themselves. The day they climb over the bars, they risk a fall from higher than an ordinary bed: that is why the Réseau Morphée recommends removing the bars at the first attempts to climb. In its article on sleep equipment, mpedia places the use of the cot between 0 and 3 years, with sides at least 60 cm high. A child who feels cramped, who asks for "a bed like the big ones", or whose cot is needed for a new baby are other common reasons.

**Why not rush.** An open bed gives the child the freedom to get up, at night as at nap time. Before age 2, many children are not yet mature enough to stay lying down; and the bedding of a "big bed" (duvet, pillow) is not advised before that age according to mpedia, which recommends the sleeping bag for every night and nap until age 2. So there is no hurry as long as your child is not climbing and sleeps well.

**A bed that grows with the child.** The Réseau Morphée points out that the bed should evolve with the child, that high beds are not advised before age 6 and should be avoided in case of sleepwalking. The DGCCRF (French consumer protection authority) fact sheet states that sleeping in a high bed is not suitable for children under 6 and that they must be kept from getting into one. For a first "big bed", choose a low bed, as close to the floor as possible.

## Day to day
**Choosing the right moment**
- Avoid piling up changes: arrival of a baby, starting nursery or school, moving house, toilet training. If a newborn needs the cot, make the move several weeks before the birth, or temporarily keep a Moses basket or cradle for the baby (mpedia places the use of a cradle up to about 6 months).
- Choose a period when your child is sleeping well and is not ill.

**Preparing the bedroom**
- A low bed, a firm mattress that fits the frame (mpedia). A small removable bed rail, or a mattress laid on the floor along the bed, cushions the first falls.
- Make safe everything your child could reach while up at night: windows, sockets, furniture that could tip over, curtain cords. Nothing should be able to wrap around the neck.
- Keep the room dark, with a very dim nightlight if needed (Réseau Morphée). Screens have no place in the bedroom.

**Supporting the transition**
- Involve your child: choosing the duvet cover, settling the cuddly toy, "inaugurating" the bed with a nap.
- Keep the **same bedtime routine**, short and identical every evening (story, cuddle, song, cuddly toy): that is what reassures, not the bed (Réseau Morphée).
- If they get up, walk them calmly back to bed, without negotiating or long discussion, as many times as needed. The Réseau Morphée advises staying firm and praising progress.
- Some parents leave the cot in the room next to the new bed for a few days; others prefer to remove it straight away. Follow your child''s reaction.

## Frequently asked questions
**My 20-month-old is already climbing over the bars.**
The Réseau Morphée advises removing the bars as soon as the child climbs, because a fall from the top of the bars is dangerous. Move to a low bed with a mattress on the floor beside it, and make the bedroom safe. Mention it to the doctor at the next visit if you are unsure.

**Since the new bed, they come and join us every night.**
This is common in the first weeks. The Réseau Morphée recommends that everyone sleeps in their own bed as far as possible, and that the child is taken back to their room with the same calm gesture every time. Brief visits at increasing intervals help the child fall back to sleep on their own.

**An extendable bed or a single bed?**
Both are fine, provided the sleeping surface is low and stable, with no gap where the child could get trapped. Avoid high beds before age 6.

**What about the duvet?**
After age 2, a light duvet or blanket can replace the sleeping bag; before that, mpedia recommends sticking to the sleeping bag.

## When to seek advice
Talk to the doctor or the PMI (mother-and-child health service) nurse if nights get lastingly worse after the change of bed, if your child wakes screaming and cannot be consoled, if they get up and wander at night without seeming awake, or if you have any doubt about the safety of their room. After a fall with loss of consciousness, vomiting or drowsiness, call **15** or **112**.

_This article is for information only; it does not replace the advice of a healthcare professional (doctor, midwife, dentist, paediatrician, psychologist) or, for administrative procedures, that of your town hall (mairie) or a legal adviser._' where slug = 'passer-du-lit-a-barreaux-au-lit-de-grand-quand-comment';
update public.articles set title_en = 'Roseola (exanthem subitum): three days of fever, then a rash', summary_en = 'A sudden fever lasting 3 to 4 days in a baby, then small pink spots as it drops: how to recognise roseola, what to watch for and when to see a doctor.', body_md_en = '## In brief
- Roseola, also called exanthem subitum or "sixth disease", is a common viral infection in infants: in **90% of cases** it occurs **before the age of 2**, most often between 7 and 13 months (Santé.fr, Vidal).
- It is often recognised in hindsight: a **sudden fever**, between 39 and 40 °C, for **3 to 4 days**, then **small pink spots** on the tummy, chest and back just as the temperature comes down.
- The virus is **not very contagious**; there is no vaccine and no specific prevention.
- Before 3 months, any fever calls for a prompt consultation. After 3 months, see a doctor quickly if the fever goes above 40 °C, if there are convulsions or if your child''s behaviour changes.

## Understanding
**A very widespread virus.** According to Santé.fr and Vidal, roseola is caused by human herpesvirus type 6. About one child in three is affected, generally between 6 months and 3 years; it is rare after the age of 4 and more frequent in spring and autumn. The virus is passed on through nose and throat secretions, but it is not very contagious: Santé.fr states that isolating the child is not necessary.

**Two quite distinct stages.**
1. *The fever.* It rises suddenly, often to between 39 and 40 °C, and lasts 3 to 4 days. Santé.fr notes that the child generally copes with it well, although they may be irritable and have swollen, tender glands. It often looks like a simple bout of fever with no explanation, and it frequently resists fever-reducing treatment, which worries parents.
2. *The rash.* Around day 4, the temperature returns to normal and small pink spots of 3 to 5 mm appear on the tummy, chest and back. They turn white when pressed, last 12 to 36 hours and may even go unnoticed.

It is this sequence, fever followed by a rash as the fever breaks, that allows the doctor to recognise roseola, most often once the spots have appeared.

**Complications are exceptional**: Vidal mentions viral meningitis, hepatitis or a drop in blood platelets. In pregnant women, Santé.fr indicates that roseola does not appear to have any adverse effect on the pregnancy, apart from the discomfort of the symptoms.

## Day to day
**During the fever**
- Monitor the temperature and write it down with the time, so you can tell the doctor.
- Offer drinks often: Santé.fr advises giving the child plenty to drink.
- Do not wrap them up too warmly and avoid letting them get too hot (overheated room, thick clothing).
- Antibiotics are of no use, since this is a virus. If fever-reducing treatment would help your child''s comfort, it is the doctor or pharmacist who tells you which one and how to use it according to their age and weight; Santé.fr points out that some fever medicines should be avoided in children.
- Watch their behaviour: a baby who smiles, drinks and responds between two peaks of fever tells you more than a number on the thermometer.

**During the rash**
- There is nothing to apply: the spots disappear by themselves in one to two days.
- A lukewarm bath and loose clothing are enough.
- Childcare: according to Santé.fr, isolation is not necessary. Still, check the rules for admitting a feverish child with the nursery or childminder.

**For brothers and sisters**: the virus is not very contagious; washing hands and toys that go in the mouth remains a good habit.

## Frequently asked questions
**My baby had 3 days of fever with no other signs, then spots. Should I see a doctor now that everything is fine?**
If a doctor saw your child during the fever, mention the rash at the next visit or by phone. If nobody examined them, an opinion is still useful to confirm that it really was roseola and not another rash illness.

**The fever will not come down despite treatment. Is that serious?**
Vidal indicates that the fever of roseola often resists treatment, without this necessarily being dangerous. What matters is how your child is: whether they are drinking, responding, free of convulsions. If in any doubt, call the doctor or 15.

**My child goes to nursery. Should they stay at home?**
Santé.fr indicates that isolation is not necessary. In practice, a feverish child is often more comfortable at home, and each setting applies its own rules.

**I am pregnant and my older child has roseola.**
Santé.fr indicates that roseola does not appear to have any adverse effect during pregnancy. Mention it all the same to your midwife or doctor.

## When to seek advice
See a doctor promptly if your baby is **under 3 months old** and has a fever, whatever the figure. After 3 months, see a doctor without delay if the fever goes above **40 °C**, in case of **convulsions**, confusion, vomiting, refusal to drink or unusual drowsiness (Santé.fr, Vidal). A rash that worries you, that spreads or that comes with a very listless child also calls for medical advice. In a life-threatening emergency, call **15** (Samu, France''s emergency medical service) or **112**.

_This article is for information only; it does not replace the advice of a healthcare professional (doctor, midwife, dentist, paediatrician, psychologist) or, for administrative procedures, that of your town hall (mairie) or a legal adviser._' where slug = 'roseole-exantheme-subit-fievre-puis-eruption-que-faire';
update public.articles set title_en = 'Measles: recognising it, understanding how contagious it is, and protecting your child', summary_en = 'A cold, red eyes, a cough and a high fever, then a rash spreading from the face down to the feet: the signs of measles, its extreme contagiousness, the MMR vaccine and the situations that require seeing a doctor.', body_md_en = '## In brief
- Measles starts like a heavy cold with **conjunctivitis**, a **cough**, great tiredness and a **high fever** (up to 39–40 °C), then a **rash** appears behind the ears and on the face before spreading down to the feet (ameli).
- It is **extremely contagious**: one patient can infect 15 to 20 people, from **5 days before to 5 days after** the rash begins (ameli, Vaccination Info Service).
- Complications occur in about **30% of cases** according to ameli, especially in babies under one year, adolescents and adults.
- The **MMR** vaccine is compulsory for children born since 1 January 2018: one dose at **12 months**, a second between **16 and 18 months**. Cases have been rising again in France since 2023.

## Understanding
**The timeline.** According to ameli, incubation lasts about ten days. Then comes a phase of 3 to 4 days that looks like a respiratory infection: runny nose, red and watery eyes bothered by light, cough, great tiredness, high fever. Before the rash, small whitish spots "looking like grains of sand" may appear inside the cheeks: this is **Koplik''s sign**, typical but not always present and often hard to see (Santé.fr).

The rash is made up of small, slightly raised red spots separated by normal skin. It is **descending**: behind the ears, on the forehead and cheeks, then the neck, upper body, and the feet by about day 3. It fades in about a week. The cold and the conjunctivitis stop, but the cough, often intense, can persist for 2 to 3 weeks (Santé.fr). Recovery takes about ten days and tiredness can last several weeks; a person who has recovered is then immune for life (ameli, Vaccination Info Service).

**Contagion.** The virus spreads through the air and the droplets produced by coughing, sneezing or blowing the nose, through close contact and through contaminated objects (toys, tissues). Contagiousness peaks from 5 days before the rash and lasts until 5 days after. Without herd immunity, one case infects 15 to 20 others: Santé.fr points out that this is about ten times more than seasonal flu. Measles is a **notifiable disease**: the doctor reports it to the health authorities to protect those around the patient.

**Complications.** Ameli describes them in about 30% of cases: ear infections and diarrhoea, lung involvement in 6% of cases, neurological involvement in 1 case in 1,000 that can leave lasting damage or cause death. The most severe forms affect children under one and adults over 20. Santé.fr adds that measles can weaken the immune defences for a long time. During pregnancy, it exposes the mother to severe lung involvement and carries a risk of premature birth.

**The situation in France.** According to ameli, 117 cases were reported in 2023, 483 in 2024 (30% hospitalised) and 873 in 2025 (36% hospitalised). In two cases out of three, vaccination was absent or incomplete.

## Day to day
**Checking your child''s protection**
- Open the carnet de santé (the child''s health record book): two doses of MMR are expected, at 12 months and then between 16 and 18 months. If the first dose was given before 12 months, a third dose is needed (Vaccination Info Service).
- Everyone born since 1980 should have received two doses, at least one month apart. An adult who cannot find their record should consider themselves unprotected and get vaccinated.
- The vaccine is covered at 100% from age 1 to 17.

**If your child has been in contact with someone who has measles**
- Call the doctor without waiting: a dose of vaccine **within 3 days** of contact can prevent the disease. For a baby under 12 months, a dose can be offered within 72 hours, followed by the two usual doses from 12 months.
- If vaccination is impossible (pregnancy, weakened immune system), other measures exist within 6 days: talk to the doctor.

**If measles is suspected**
- Stay at home, avoid visitors and **call the doctor before going anywhere** (Vaccination Info Service), so as not to infect a waiting room.
- There is no specific treatment: rest, drinks, and the comfort measures the doctor advises. Antibiotics do not work on this virus.
- Tell the nursery, the school and vulnerable relatives (pregnant woman, unvaccinated baby, immunocompromised person).

## Frequently asked questions
**My baby is 9 months old and we are travelling to an area where measles is circulating.**
Vaccination Info Service indicates that vaccination is possible from 6 months in this case, with doses at 12 and 16–18 months afterwards. Ask the doctor for advice before you leave.

**Can the vaccine give measles?**
The most common effects are pain at the injection site and sometimes a fever in the following days. An allergic reaction is very rare (1 case in 450,000). A link with autism has never been confirmed: the 1998 study behind this fear was retracted, and a 2019 Danish study of 650,000 children shows no increase in risk.

**I do not know whether I had measles.**
Only a definite history of the disease means no vaccine is needed. Vaccination Info Service specifies that the vaccine can be given even to someone who has already had the disease or already received doses.

## When to seek advice
Call the doctor the same day if there is a high fever with a cough, red eyes and a rash, especially if your child is not vaccinated or is under one year old, or after contact with a case. Seek emergency care if your child is breathing fast or with difficulty, is very drowsy or confused, has a seizure, has a severe headache or refuses to drink. A pregnant woman in contact with measles should quickly contact her midwife or doctor. In a life-threatening emergency, call **15** (Samu, France''s emergency medical service) or **112**.

_This article is for information only; it does not replace the advice of a healthcare professional (doctor, midwife, dentist, paediatrician, psychologist) or, for administrative procedures, that of your town hall (mairie) or a legal adviser._' where slug = 'rougeole-reconnaitre-contagiosite-vaccin-ror-quand-consulter';
update public.articles set title_en = 'Washing from 1 to 10 years: hands, shower, hair, ears, nails and learning to wash alone', summary_en = 'A bath or shower is not essential every day, hair every 2 or 3 days, never a cotton bud in the ear canal: the landmarks from paediatricians and the Assurance Maladie for simple hygiene, and the steps towards your child becoming independent.', body_md_en = '## In brief
- Between 1 and 3 years, no special care is needed: the bath or shower is above all a chance to learn, little by little, to be independent and to enjoy being clean (mpedia).
- A bath is **not essential every day**; hair is washed **every 2 or 3 days** with a mild shampoo.
- Ears are cleaned **at the entrance of the canal only**: no cotton buds, which push the wax in and can form a plug or cause injury (mpedia, ameli).
- Hands are washed **after the toilet, before meals** and when dirty: a step stool in front of the washbasin helps the child do it alone.
- **Never leave a child alone in the bath**, even at 2 or 3 years old, even for a moment.

## Understanding
**Simple hygiene is enough.** A child''s skin does not need lots of products. For babies, Santé.fr advises a simple soap, unscented and non-antibacterial, and limiting wipes; these principles still hold as the child grows. Mpedia points out that a daily bath is not essential: the older the child, the dirtier they get while playing, and the bath becomes above all a moment of relaxation they enjoy. For dry skin or eczema, the mpedia paediatrician advises spacing out baths (one every two or three days), a superfatted soap and an emollient cream after getting out of the water.

**Ears clean themselves.** According to ameli, earwax is a protective substance for the ear that does not need removing. Only the visible part of the canal may be cleaned, with a damp cloth over a fingertip, without pushing it in. Cotton buds, like any other instrument, should be avoided: they can injure the canal or push the wax back and form a plug. Mpedia suggests the jet of the shower head or a pharmacy spray, once to several times a week.

**Independence is learnt.** Between 1 and 3 years, the child takes part: soaping their tummy, holding out their hands, choosing the towel. When they start washing alone, mpedia advises showing them how, because they easily forget parts of the body. Full independence (showering, washing hair, cutting nails) comes gradually during primary school, at very different rates from one child to another.

## Day to day
**Hands.** Water and soap, rubbing palms, backs, between the fingers and under the nails, then dry well. Before meals, after the toilet, after coming in from outside, after touching an animal or blowing the nose. A step stool and soap within reach make the gesture possible without help (mpedia).

**Bath or shower.**
- A pleasant temperature, checked with the elbow or a bath thermometer; bath water cools quickly, so add hot water while keeping the child away from the tap (mpedia).
- A non-slip mat or stick-on shapes on the bottom of the bath.
- Never alone, even for a moment: "a child can drown in very little water and without making the slightest sound" (mpedia). Get everything ready beforehand, and ignore the phone.
- Around 5–6 years, many children prefer the shower; stay nearby until they are comfortable with the tap.

**Face.** With water and soap, helping the child; teach them not to forget the neck and behind the ears.

**Hair.** A mild shampoo, possibly a children''s one, every 2 or 3 days. A bath visor or tilting the head back limits soap in the eyes.

**Ears.** The entrance of the canal only, with a damp cloth or the shower jet. No cotton bud in the canal, for children or adults.

**Nails.** Check their length at every wash and cut them regularly with round-ended scissors, leaving no sharp points at the sides (mpedia). Around 8–10 years, show your child how to do it themselves.

**Intimate hygiene.** Surface cleaning, from front to back, with water and mild soap, then dry. For girls, Santé.fr says not to rub between the labia; for uncircumcised boys, not to pull back the foreskin.

**Learning to wash alone, step by step**
1. Between 1 and 3 years: they take part (soaping themselves, rinsing with the shower head, drying with you).
2. Between 3 and 6 years: they wash under your eye, you check the "forgotten" areas (neck, armpits, feet, bottom) and you do the hair.
3. From 6–7 years: showering alone with the door open, shampoo with help and then alone; you remain available.
4. Around 9–10 years: a full wash alone, including nails and hair; a reminder of the basics is still useful as puberty approaches.

## Frequently asked questions
**My child hates shampoo.**
Space it out (every 2 or 3 days is enough), use a visor or a flannel over the eyes, let them hold the shower head and pour the water themselves. Mpedia suggests games, songs and water toys to make the moment enjoyable.

**They have visible wax in the ear.**
Clean only what is visible at the entrance, with a damp cloth. If a plug persists or there is pain, see the doctor: ameli specifies that a plug that is not removed can grow, harden or irritate the canal.

**At what age can they stay alone in the bathroom?**
There is no official age. While the child is in the water and until they master the tap and their balance, an adult stays present or within earshot, door open.

## When to seek advice
See the doctor if the skin becomes red, dry, cracked or itchy despite gentle hygiene, in case of ear pain or discharge, a persistent wax plug, an ingrown nail, persistent redness of the private parts, or if your child persistently refuses all washing. After an object has been put in the ear, do not try to remove it yourself and seek medical advice. In case of drowning or faintness in the bath, call **15** or **112**.

_This article is for information only; it does not replace the advice of a healthcare professional (doctor, midwife, dentist, paediatrician, psychologist) or, for administrative procedures, that of your town hall (mairie) or a legal adviser._' where slug = 'toilette-quotidienne-enfant-1-a-10-ans-mains-douche-cheveux-oreilles-ongles';
update public.articles set title_en = 'Hives (urticaria) in children: itchy welts, and the signs that should alert you', summary_en = 'Raised pink welts that move around and itch: what urticaria is, its most common causes in children (often an infection), and the warning signs that mean you must call 15.', body_md_en = '## In brief
- Urticaria is recognised by **raised pink welts**, rounded, well defined, that itch, **move around** and disappear within 48 hours without leaving a mark (ameli).
- In children, the most frequent cause of acute urticaria is an **infection** (cold, tonsillitis, ear infection, bronchitis); a true allergy is **rare**.
- A single flare most often clears by itself within a few hours. Urticaria that lasts more than a week, spreads over the whole body or comes with a fever calls for a consultation.
- **Call 15** if the lips, tongue or eyelids swell, if your child has trouble breathing or swallowing, or if they faint.

## Understanding
**What happens in the skin.** According to ameli, urticaria is caused by the activation of immune cells in the skin, the mast cells, which release histamine. The French name comes from the nettle (ortie): the welts look like nettle stings. One person in five has at least one episode of acute urticaria in their lifetime.

**Acute or chronic.** **Acute** urticaria is a single flare, lasting a few hours to a few days, which may recur; most often it is not allergic. **Chronic** urticaria is defined by flares every day or every 2 to 3 days for at least six weeks; it is a non-allergic inflammatory skin disease that affects about 1% of the population and eventually disappears over several months or years.

**Causes in children.** Ameli lists first ENT and respiratory infections, mostly viral, and intestinal parasites. At all ages, other triggers exist: some medicines (15 to 20% of acute urticaria), histamine-rich foods (fermented cheeses, cured meats, tinned foods, tomato, spinach), contact (nettles, latex, cleaning products, jellyfish), physical causes (rubbing, pressure, cold, heat, sun, exertion, emotions) and stress. True allergies (foods, medicines, latex, insect venom) are rare: they occur within two hours of contact and can be serious.

**Angioedema.** When the swelling affects the deeper skin or the mucous membranes, especially the face (eyelids, lips), it is called angioedema. In the great majority of cases it is harmless. More rarely it reaches the throat: this is **Quincke''s oedema**, which can obstruct breathing and requires hospital admission.

## Day to day
**During a flare**
- Observe the welts: note when they appeared, where they are and what your child ate, took or did in the previous hours. This information helps the doctor.
- Photograph the rash: it has often disappeared by the time of the appointment.
- Dress your child in loose cotton clothing; avoid very hot baths, which increase itching.
- Keep their nails short to limit scratch marks.
- Ameli indicates that topical creams can soothe, with often limited effectiveness: ask the pharmacist or doctor before applying any product, and never use another family member''s medicine.

**Checking that it really is urticaria**
At a first rash, ameli advises having the diagnosis confirmed, because urticaria can look like eczema. Urticaria welts move and fade within 48 hours; a lesion that stays in the same place for several days is probably not urticaria.

**After the flare**
- If a cause has been identified (food, medicine, contact), avoid it, and tell the school, the nursery and any professional who prescribes for your child.
- Urticaria that occurs during a cold or tonsillitis does not require an allergy work-up: the doctor decides whether it is useful.

## Frequently asked questions
**My child has welts after a meal. Is it an allergy?**
Not necessarily: some foods trigger urticaria without allergy, and an ongoing infection is the most common cause in children. A true allergy occurs within two hours and may come with other signs (vomiting, swelling, breathing difficulty): in that case, call 15.

**Is it contagious?**
Urticaria itself is not passed on. The infection that caused it may be.

**The welts come back every evening and have for ten days.**
Ameli advises seeing the family doctor when urticaria lasts more than a week or recurs, to look for the cause and propose suitable treatment.

**Can they go to school?**
If the child is well and has no fever, there is generally no objection; it is the associated infection that may justify staying at home.

## When to seek advice
According to ameli, see the doctor **the same day** if the rash spreads over the whole body, if it comes with a fever, or if your child has urticaria with a persistent cough. See the doctor **in the coming days** if the welts form large patches, if the itching persists for more than 24 hours despite the advice received, if the urticaria lasts more than a week or recurs, if the joints are swollen and painful, or if your child has asthma. Call **15** or **112** without delay if the lips, tongue or eyelids swell with a burning sensation, if they have trouble breathing or swallowing, or if the rash comes with faintness, pallor, sweating or loss of consciousness. In a life-threatening emergency, call **15** (Samu, France''s emergency medical service) or **112**.

_This article is for information only; it does not replace the advice of a healthcare professional (doctor, midwife, dentist, paediatrician, psychologist) or, for administrative procedures, that of your town hall (mairie) or a legal adviser._' where slug = 'urticaire-enfant-plaques-demangeaisons-causes-signes-alerte';
update public.articles set title_en = 'Vulvitis and intimate irritation in little girls: simple hygiene and the signs that call for a doctor', summary_en = 'Redness, itching or burning of the vulva are common in little girls before puberty: why, how washing and clothing help, and which signs (foul-smelling discharge, pain when passing urine, worms) call for medical advice.', body_md_en = '## In brief
- Vulvovaginitis is common in little girls before puberty: ameli explains it by the **fragility of the mucous membranes** and underdeveloped labia minora, which make it easier for germs from the anus to pass through.
- The most frequent causes are **incomplete washing or irritating soaps**, a urinary infection, **threadworms** (pinworms) or, more rarely, a foreign body.
- **Gentle, surface washing, from front to back**, without rubbing between the labia, and cotton underwear are most often enough (Santé.fr, ameli).
- **Foul-smelling discharge** should prompt a quick consultation; discomfort that persists despite these measures calls for medical advice.

## Understanding
**Why it is common at this age.** Before puberty, the lining of the vulva is thin and poorly protected. Ameli specifies that infectious vulvovaginitis is common in little girls, favoured by the fragility of the mucous membranes and by underdeveloped labia minora, which let germs from the anal area through more easily. Vaginitis is almost always associated with irritation of the vulva, hence the term vulvovaginitis.

**Causes specific to little girls** (ameli):
- **incomplete washing** or, conversely, **irritating soaps** and excessive hygiene;
- a **urinary infection**;
- **threadworms**: the small white worms lay their eggs around the anus at night, the child scratches and, in girls, this can cause vulvitis, sometimes with difficulty passing urine or leaks (ameli, page on threadworms);
- a **foreign body** inserted in the vagina (paper, cotton wool, a marble, a crayon), which often causes a foul-smelling discharge and requires an urgent consultation.

**What you may notice.** Redness of the vulva, itching or burning, swollen and tender labia, sometimes discharge, discomfort when passing urine, a child who scratches or complains when wiping. Ameli describes, depending on the cause, thick whitish discharge, thin and foul-smelling discharge, or none at all in the case of simple irritation.

**Factors that keep the irritation going**: tight or synthetic clothing, bubble baths and scented products, vaginal douching (to be avoided altogether), the dampness of a swimsuit kept on for a long time, scented toilet paper, wiping from back to front.

## Day to day
**Washing**
- Once a day, with lukewarm water and a mild unscented soap, **on the surface**: Santé.fr says not to rub between the labia. Rinse and pat dry.
- After a bowel movement, clean with water and soap then rinse well, always **from the genitals towards the bottom** (Santé.fr).
- No bubble bath, no scented gel, no wipes for everyday use.

**At the toilet**
- Teach your daughter to wipe **from front to back**, and not to hold in urine.
- Knees apart and feet on a step stool so that urine does not flow back towards the vulva.

**Clothing**
- **Cotton** knickers, changed every day; no tights or trousers that are too tight all day long.
- Take off a wet swimsuit quickly after the pool or the beach.
- Sleeping without knickers under pyjamas can help air the area when it is irritated.

**In case of itching**
- Avoid "intimate" creams or products without advice: some irritate even more. The pharmacist or doctor will guide you.
- Short nails to limit scratch marks.
- If the itching is mainly at night and also affects the anus, think of threadworms: ameli indicates that parents can look for small white moving worms in the stools, on the anus or in the underwear, and see a doctor if in doubt.

## Frequently asked questions
**My 5-year-old has white discharge. Is it worrying?**
Scant discharge, with no smell and no redness or pain, may be seen. Thick, coloured, foul-smelling discharge, or discharge with burning, calls for a consultation, as it points to an infection or a foreign body (ameli).

**Do we need a special intimate wash?**
No: a mild unscented soap is enough. Ameli lists intimate washes among the products that can irritate.

**It hurts when she wees.**
Pain when passing urine can come from irritation of the vulva, but also from a urinary infection, which ameli lists among the causes of vulvovaginitis in little girls. See the doctor, who may ask for a urine test.

**Can she go swimming?**
Yes, taking off the wet swimsuit as soon as she gets out and rinsing with clean water. If irritation is present, wait until it has healed.

## When to seek advice
See the doctor or paediatrician if the redness and itching persist after a few days of gentle washing, in case of thick or foul-smelling discharge, pain or burning when passing urine, fever, bleeding, visible small worms, or if you suspect a foreign body (an urgent consultation according to ameli). Any unexplained lesion, or an associated change in behaviour, should also be reported to the doctor. If you think a child is in danger, call **119** (Allô Enfance en danger, France''s child protection helpline).

_This article is for information only; it does not replace the advice of a healthcare professional (doctor, midwife, dentist, paediatrician, psychologist) or, for administrative procedures, that of your town hall (mairie) or a legal adviser._' where slug = 'vulvite-irritations-intimes-petite-fille-hygiene-quand-consulter';
